<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alaknanda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alaknanda is a Himalayan river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and one of the two headstreams of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. Rising near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a>, it flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> and joins the Bhagirathi at Devprayag to form the Ganges. Its valley carries the pilgrim route to Badrinath.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaknanda.jpg" alt="Alaknanda" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fowler&amp;fowler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaknanda_devprayag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Alaknanda is one of the two headstreams of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, the major river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and a river held holy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. In hydrology it counts as the source stream of the Ganges because of its greater length and discharge and because it contributes a significantly larger share of the flow than the Bhagirathi, while in Hindu tradition the Bhagirathi is considered the source. It rises at the foot of the Satopanth and Bhagirath Kharak glaciers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, and its system drains parts of the Chamoli, Tehri and Pauri districts.</p><p>From its source the river travels to the village of Mana and meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> at Keshav Prayag, then runs through narrow valleys to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a> valley and Hanumanchatti, where the Ghrit Ganga joins it. After Pandukeshwar it flows through wider valleys and steep terrain, and at Vishnuprayag the Dhauliganga joins it. It travels west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joshimath">Joshimath</a>, crosses the Main Central Thrust near Helang, and takes in the Birahi Ganga at Birahi. At Nandprayag it meets the Nandakini, at Karanprayag the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, and at Rudraprayag the Mandakini, before it enters a wide valley near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a>. At Devprayag the Alaknanda meets the Bhagirathi and from there goes on as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts the Alaknanda is used as a synonym for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, and in the Vishnu Purana the Ganges divides into four rivers, one of them called Alaknanda, which flows south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Five confluences along it, called prayags, are places of pilgrimage: Vishnuprayag, Nandaprayag, Karnaprayag, Rudraprayag and Devprayag, together the Panch Prayag. The river also passes the pilgrimage places of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joshimath">Joshimath</a>. It is known for river rafting, and 37 hydroelectric dams on the river and its tributaries are in operation, under construction or planned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alaknanda River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ajay Pal moved the capital to Devalgarh and then to Srinagar on the <b>Alaknanda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/garhwal-kingdom">Garhwal Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
